sub:Head {
  this: a np:Nanopublication ;
    np:hasAssertion sub:assertion ;
    np:hasProvenance sub:provenance ;
    np:hasPublicationInfo sub:pubinfo .
}

sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/hypothesis/truly-flying-fish-requirements-sixty-gram> a delphi:Hypothesis, skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "HYPOTHETICAL: a 60 g flying fish that climbs under its own power would need four times the fin area, sixteen percent flight muscle and about five times a bat's oxygen supply per kilogram" ;
    dct:description "Design study, not an organism. Keeping the real specimen's mass (0.06 kg), fin section (C_Lmax 0.78, L/D max 4.37, Park and Choi 2010) and the simulator's equations, and changing only morphology and physiology: wing loading 12 N/m2 (inside the bat range 6.5 to 14.25) needs lifting area 0.0491 m2, 3.9 times the real reference area, span 0.66 m at aspect ratio 9. Level flight at the best speed costs 1.28 W mechanical including 0.48 W of flapping inertia; a 1 m/s climb 1.86 W. At a muscle specific power of 202 W/kg derived from a bat (Pipistrellus nathusii: 0.140 W maximum mechanical power from 0.69 g of flight muscle), the bat mean muscle fraction (9.13 percent) gives 1.1 W, not enough for level flight; 16 percent gives 1.94 W and covers the climb. At 10 percent conversion efficiency (bat-measured 7.0 to 10.4) the climb needs 18.6 W metabolic, 311 W per kg body, about 928 ml O2 per minute per kg, while airborne, where gills do not ventilate: an air-breathing respiratory surface is therefore a design requirement, not an option. Status: untested hypothesis (design study); the oxygen side awaits the physiology chain and the Lean O2-to-power bound, and the simulated climb is power-limited only." ;
    delphi:context "Claim under test: thompson1917-ch02-06 (D Arcy Thompson 1917, On Growth and Form, ch. II, Project Gutenberg 55264 lines 1716-1790), with thompson1917-ch02-07 and thompson1917-ch02-10. Taxon: Exocoetidae, NCBI Taxonomy 76072, GBIF 2968. Class Aves is the frozen holdout (holdout/exam.json); no bird value enters this record." ;
    delphi:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/physics-flier-design-budget> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-024-06032-9>, <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2023.0045>, <https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2022.0315>, <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-92499-z>, <https://doi.org/10.1098/rsbl.2025.0325>, <https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.046052> ;
    skos:scopeNote "Hypothetical throughout; every input is a bat, insect or pterosaur value or a measured flying-fish value; the muscle specific power is derived from two sourced bat numbers." ;
    rdfs:comment "Status: inconclusive. Does not establish: that such a fish could develop (development is not simulated); that fins of four times the area keep the measured polar; that any respiratory surface can deliver the oxygen in air; that the derived muscle specific power applies to fish muscle; anything about birds. It is a statement of requirements under the stated equations, not a prediction that the organism is possible." ;
    rdfs:comment "Pre-registration: physics/tests/thompson1917-ch02-06/result.json, written 2026-10-31T18:21:00Z before any wing loading, lifting area or lift coefficient was read, amended 2026-10-31T18:26:00Z before any fit ran (the file lists both and the values seen before each); sha256 at build 0094c084db22278a5f98d96cc2971ccd27813b0acdc458d60cadf7c7b7bcae48. The git commit that timestamps it is pending: the physics thread does not commit; the hub does." ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch06-05> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://delphi-mission-control.ashenoy000.workers.dev/t/physics/>, <https://delphi-mission-control.ashenoy000.workers.dev/t/physics/results.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/physics-flier-design-budget> a delphi:EvidenceItem ;
    rdfs:label "Power and oxygen budget of the designed flier from sourced non-bird values" ;
    dct:description "Inputs (physics/flier/spec.json, sources block): bat flight muscle fraction mean 9.13 percent, minimum 7.8 (10.1038/s42003-024-06032-9 citing Bullen and McKenzie 2004); P. nathusii mechanical power 0.046 to 0.140 W for 7.6 g and conversion efficiency 7.0 to 10.4 percent (10.1098/rspb.2023.0045); inertial power 8 W/kg (10.1098/rsif.2022.0315); bat wing loadings 6.5 to 14.25 N/m2 (compilation); pterosaur hatchling wing loading 15.8 to 17.9 N/m2 and aspect ratio 6.1 to 6.5 (10.1038/s41598-021-92499-z); bumblebee hovering 559 W/kg, efficiency 7.3 to 14.7 percent (10.1098/rsbl.2025.0325); oxycaloric equivalent 20.1 J per ml O2. No bird value: class Aves is the frozen holdout. Computed by physics/flier/design.py. In the simulator the flier lifts off at 11.6 m/s and climbs to 42 m in 40 s with the real fish's equations, on muscle power alone with no oxygen limit applied." ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/hypothesis/truly-flying-fish-requirements-sixty-gram> ;
    rdfs:comment "Computed by physics/analysis.py from physics/glide-data.csv (sha256 bc24245b5163723b192e95fccf714ef6e4b3b28e71df4fef3befbaf008a00635) and physics/comparison-data.csv (sha256 e0d3d74639c1fe856fe5128c1783151596fde3acc923a2409be3d3f870cc394a); numbers in physics/results.json (sha256 63336841c0d4c9ae2760ae5c87a9251b97218e3ebfdd453cad4cffb9cdf6ba0b). Every input value carries its source, locator, retrieval time and value kind in those files." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://delphi-mission-control.ashenoy000.workers.dev/t/physics/results.json>, <https://delphi-mission-control.ashenoy000.workers.dev/t/physics/data/glide-data.csv>, <https://delphi-mission-control.ashenoy000.workers.dev/t/physics/data/comparison-data.csv> .
}

